            <ArticleTitle>Coronary flow reserve assessed by transesophageal echocardiography identifies early restenosis of the left anterior descending coronary artery angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>803-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although an increase in diastolic coronary flow velocity can be detected by transesophageal echocardiography 72 hours after both successful and unsuccessful left anterior coronary artery angioplasty, a significant improvement in coronary flow reserve is observed only in patients with a successful procedure. Transesophageal echocardiography-derived coronary flow reserve can identify early restenosis and thus serve as an index of the outcome of the procedure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
